# DOM-Based XSS 🌲

In DOM-based XSS, the attack payload is processed **entirely within the browser client** by JavaScript. The server might not even receive or see the payload!

## How DOM XSS Happens: Sources & Sinks

- **Source**: An input that reads untrusted data (e.g. `location.search`, `location.hash`, `document.referrer`).
- **Sink**: A dangerous JavaScript API that executes strings as HTML or code.

## Vulnerable Code Example ❌

```javascript_not
// URL: https://site.com/welcome.html#<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
const hash = decodeURIComponent(window.location.hash.substring(1));

// VULNERABLE SINK: innerHTML parses the string as raw DOM markup!
document.getElementById('greeting').innerHTML = "Hello, " + hash;
```

# Rich Text Sanitization with DOMPurify 🧼

What if your application *must* allow users to format comments with bold text (`<b>`), links (`<a>`), and lists (`<ul>`)?

You cannot use raw HTML entity encoding. You need an **HTML Sanitizer**.

## Enter DOMPurify

**DOMPurify** parses HTML into a DOM tree, strips all executable tags (`<script>`, `<iframe>`), dangerous attributes (`onerror`, `onclick`), and pseudo-protocols (`javascript:`):

```javascript_not
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

const dirtyHtml = "<p>Welcome! <img src=x onerror=alert(1)> <b>User</b></p>";
const cleanHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirtyHtml);

// Output: <p>Welcome! <img src="x"> <b>User</b></p>
document.getElementById('content').innerHTML = cleanHtml; // SAFE!
```
